  "query_preview": "..\n\nA blocking pattern will result in surface high pressure and ridging \nbeing entrenched for most of this week across the Great Lakes \nyielding quiet weather conditions. \n\nSurface high pressure slides into Quebec with a light east-\nsoutheasterly wind component prevailing into tonight. A similar set \nup tonight compared to last night with favorable radiational cooling \nconditions. The lingering lake moisture and a little more notable \nmoisture influx off Lake Erie should bring potential again for fog \ndevelopment tonight. It is difficult to pin down where exactly \ngreater coverage of patchy dense fog may develop, but models suggest \ngreater potential exists for a broader area across southeast Michigan\nto be impacted by some coverage of fog by tomorrow morning. \n\nNo major swings in the thermal profiles for the week with \npersistence of a warmer airmass that brings temperatures that are a \nfew degrees above climatological norms. Afternoon highs will be \nmainly in the 60s with overnight lows ranging from the mid-upper 30s \nto mid 40s. \n\nThe northern stream across central Canada will send a front into the \nregion that will weaken and stall out as it moves into the Great \nLakes tomorrow while longwave ridge amplification ramps back up \nensuring. Lack of any deeper moisture will allow dry and around or \nslightly above normal warmth into the weekend. High pressure centered\nover eastern Canada will offer potential for slightly cooler \ntemperatures Friday down to around 60 with the northerly flow \nbecoming established for the day. Temperatures quickly recover as \nthermal ridge builds back into the Great Lakes for the weekend. The \nnext indication of a pattern change is next week as long range \nensemble members point toward a stronger Canadian wave that will \nbreak down this blocking pattern and open up the door to our next \nchance a precipitation and cooler temperatures.",
